WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.198
Software supply chain security focuses on the many sources, tools, dependencies,

00:00:04.198 --> 00:00:08.296
build processes, and distribution paths involved in creating and operating software.

00:00:08.296 --> 00:00:12.394
Software rarely consists only of original code written by one team.

00:00:12.394 --> 00:00:15.002
Applications often rely on numerous external components.

00:00:15.002 --> 00:00:18.728
Those components may be maintained by open-source communities, commercial vendors,

00:00:18.728 --> 00:00:21.708
cloud providers, package repositories, or internal platform teams.

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:23.198
Every dependency introduces questions.

00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:24.316
Who maintains it?

00:00:24.316 --> 00:00:25.806
Which version is used?

00:00:25.806 --> 00:00:27.296
Is it still supported?

00:00:27.296 --> 00:00:29.532
Does it bring in additional dependencies?

00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:31.022
Where is it used?

00:00:31.022 --> 00:00:34.747
How quickly can the organization respond if a concern appears?

00:00:34.747 --> 00:00:37.728
SBOMs help organizations answer those questions more consistently.

00:00:37.728 --> 00:00:39.591
By documenting components and relationships,

00:00:39.591 --> 00:00:45.179
they improve awareness of the software supply chain and help teams understand where dependencies appear.

00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:47.414
Supply chain visibility supports incident response.

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:52.257
If a component is affected by a newly disclosed vulnerability or other concern,

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:57.473
an SBOM can help teams narrow the search and identify products that need review.

00:00:57.473 --> 00:01:00.453
SBOMs also support procurement and vendor risk conversations.

00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:03.061
Customers may ask vendors for component transparency,

00:01:03.061 --> 00:01:07.905
and buyers may use SBOM information as one input when evaluating software risk.

00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:10.512
The key idea is visibility, not fear.

00:01:10.512 --> 00:01:14.983
SBOMs help organizations understand software composition so they can manage dependencies with

00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:17.218
better information and more deliberate decisions.

00:01:17.218 --> 00:01:19.454
That visibility can also improve collaboration.

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:22.062
Developers, security teams, product owners, procurement teams,

00:01:22.062 --> 00:01:26.532
and customers can discuss the same component information instead of working from

00:01:26.532 --> 00:01:29.140
separate assumptions about what the software contains.
